            <ArticleTitle>Overlapping features of eclampsia and postpartum angiopathy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>199-209</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s12028-009-9221-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Postpartum cerebral angiopathy (PPA) is considered one of a diverse group of rare conditions termed &quot;reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndromes&quot;. Existing literature suggest considerable overlap in the manifestations of eclampsia and PPA.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective case series review of PPA and eclampsia from a single neurosciences intensive care unit patient log identified over a consecutive 18-month period. A MEDLINE search (using OVID) of the English literature from 1950 through October 2008 was also performed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Four patients who meet the obstetrical criteria for eclampsia and four patients whose clinical and radiographic features were consistent with PPA were identified. Twenty-eight patients with PPA were identified from the literature and showed significant clinical and radiographic overlap without cohort.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Given the overlapping clinical, laboratory, and radiographical features of eclampsia and PPA, it is probable they share a similar underlying pathophysiological mechanism and represent different clinical expressions of the same pregnancy-related disorder. The obstetrical definition of eclampsia may be to strict when applied in the neurosciences intensive care unit.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
